    <body>
      <sec id="article-27310.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Malaria remains a major global health burden, with an estimated 282 million cases and 610,000 deaths in 2024, predominantly in Africa. Caused by single-celled parasites of the genus <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> is the most geographically widespread and notable for its dormant liver hypnozoite stage, enabling relapsing infections months to years after initial exposure. This course details the transmission of malaria by <italic toggle="yes">Anopheles</italic> mosquitoes, the parasites' hepatic and erythrocytic phases, and the influence of these processes on clinical symptoms, such as cyclical fevers, anemia, and systemic illness. Participants will also gain an understanding of appropriate diagnostic strategies that rely on microscopy and rapid diagnostic tests and evidence-based, patient-centered management, including treatment of both blood-stage infection and latent hepatic forms, with attention to drug resistance patterns and G6PD status. This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in identifying malaria infections, performing the recommended evaluation, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach when managing this condition.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify key clinical features suggestive of <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium vivax</italic> malaria to support timely diagnosis.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Interpret laboratory findings to differentiate <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium vivax</italic> from other malaria species.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Select guideline-directed treatment regimens for <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium vivax&#x000a0;</italic>malaria, incorporating appropriate screening.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with&#x000a0;interprofessional team members to improve care coordination and outcomes for patients affected by malaria.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=27310&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=27310">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27310.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Malaria&#x000a0;is a significant global health problem with a substantial disease burden worldwide. In 2024, there were approximately 282 million cases of malaria, responsible for about 610,000&#x000a0;deaths, the majority on the African continent.[WHO. <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.who.int/teams/global-malaria-programme/reports/world-malaria-report-2025">World Malaria Report 2025</ext-link>]&#x000a0;Malaria results from infection with single-celled parasites belonging to the <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium</italic> genus. The 6 species of Plasmodium that are known to cause disease in humans include&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P falciparum</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">P ovale curtisi, P ovale wallikeri</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">P malariae</italic>, and <italic toggle="yes">P knowlesi</italic>. Globally, <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium falciparum</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium vivax</italic> account for the majority of malaria cases. While <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium falciparum</italic> is responsible for far more deaths, <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium vivax</italic> is the most geographically widespread of all of the malaria species, can cause severe, even fatal infections, and resulted in an estimated 9.9 million cases in 2024.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27310.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium vivax</italic> is a protozoal infection transmitted via the <italic toggle="yes">Anopheles</italic> mosquito.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27310.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>
<bold>Global Epidemiology&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Half of the population on earth is at risk of contracting malaria, with&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P</italic><italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;vivax</italic>&#x000a0;as the most widespread of the malaria species. Due to its dormant liver phase, <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> can survive in colder climates than other malaria species, giving it a wider geographical range, including the tropics, subtropics, and temperate regions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;The highest prevalence is in Latin America and Southeast Asia. According to the WHO, 63% of malaria cases in the Region of the Americas in 2024&#x000a0;were caused by&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P</italic><italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;vivax</italic>.[WHO. <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.who.int/teams/global-malaria-programme/reports/world-malaria-report-2025">World Malaria Report 2024</ext-link>]&#x000a0;A unique characteristic of the <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> parasite is that it requires the Duffy antigen on the surface of cells to invade red blood cells, which results in its prevalence being much lower than <italic toggle="yes">P falciparum</italic> in Africa, where the population has low expression of the Duffy antigen.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>Classic teaching that the lack of Duffy antigen&#x000a0;confers resistance to&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P vivax&#x000a0;</italic>malaria infection has been called into question by more recent studies showing&#x000a0;rare cases of <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> infection in Duffy-null Africans.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r3">[3]</xref> Acquisition of immunity to <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> occurs more rapidly than immunity to <italic toggle="yes">P falciparum</italic>. Thus, in endemic regions, morbidity tends to peak at an earlier age in <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic>, and adults are more often asymptomatic when infected. Alternatively, in low transmission settings, the risk of severe disease is not dependent on age.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Epidemiology in the United States&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>According to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), in 2022, 2000 cases of malaria were diagnosed in the United States, resulting in 10 deaths (0.5% case fatality rate).[CDC.&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.cdc.gov/malaria/php/surveillance-report/2022.html">Malaria Surveillance Data</ext-link>. 2022]&#x000a0;Approximately 80% of malaria in the United States is contracted in Africa and imported.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;In a 2004 study by Newman et al that reviewed 185 deaths between 1963 and 2001, 92.7% were due to <italic toggle="yes">P falciparum,</italic> with only 3.3% attributed to <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic>. The study found that failure to take chemoprophylaxis, taking inappropriate or incomplete prophylaxis, and missed diagnosis were common, leading to the finding that 85% of the malaria deaths were preventable.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;After a 20-year hiatus with no cases of autochthonous malaria in the United States, in 2023, outbreaks comprising 10 cases were reported; 9 of 10 were caused by <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r7">[7]</xref> The CDC authors of this paper wrote that, &#x0201c;increased travel and population movement to and from malaria-endemic regions, persistent vectors, and increased temperatures may be increasing the risk of local transmission in the United States.&#x0201d;</p>
        <p>
<bold>The Impact of COVID-19 on Malaria</bold>
</p>
        <p>Worldwide, malaria cases reported to the World Health Organization (WHO) increased steadily between 2018 and 2024 despite potential disruptions to diagnostic services caused by the COVID-19 pandemic. The COVID-19 pandemic interrupted service delivery as limited resources were redirected to address COVID-19 from 2020 to 2023.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r9">[9]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27310.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Malaria parasites are&#x000a0;protozoans belonging to the genus <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium</italic>. The definitive host of the parasite is the <italic toggle="yes">Anopheles</italic> mosquito, and vertebrates (primarily humans) serve as the parasite's intermediate hosts. Malaria parasites are transmitted from the mosquito to its human host in the form of sporozoites during a blood meal. These immediately migrate to the liver, where they invade hepatocytes and form schizonts. When these schizonts rupture, <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium</italic> merozoites are released into the blood (see <bold>Image</bold>. <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium&#x000a0;</italic>Life Cycle). This blood stage coincides with malaria symptoms in the host.</p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium vivax</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium ovale</italic> have a dormant hypnozoite stage in the liver. Hypnozoites may remain dormant for months or even years, causing relapsing infection when they re-enter the bloodstream.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r4">[4]</xref> Merozoites of <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> only infect reticulocytes, unlike other species of malaria, which will infect all stages of the red blood cell. This exclusive preference for reticulocytes results in significantly lower parasitemia levels in patients infected with&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic>&#x000a0;than in those infected with&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P</italic><italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;falciparum</italic>.&#x000a0;Although parasitemia rarely exceeds 2% to 3%,&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic>&#x000a0;can still cause significant disease due to an&#x000a0;increased host immune response.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Growing evidence suggests that much of the biomass of <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> in chronic and asymptomatic infections is intrasplenic and in the bone marrow.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;The&#x000a0;parasite's survival in the human host helps explain, in part, how&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic>&#x000a0;persists in temperate climates, eg,&#x000a0;the Korean peninsula. Parasites undergo sexual and asexual multiplication in the human host. The infection spreads when a mosquito takes a blood meal from an infected human, continuing the life cycle of the malaria parasite and eventually inoculating its next human host.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27310.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>On Giemsa-stained blood smears,&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic>&#x000a0;parasites may be present. Because&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic>&#x000a0;preferentially invades reticulocytes, typical smears show less than 1% parasitemia. Unlike <italic toggle="yes">P falciparum</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> causes red blood cells to appear larger than uninfected cells, and trophozoites may appear as thick, large ameboid rings, about one-half the diameter of the RBC, with Sch&#x000fc;ffner dots or stippling present (see<bold> Image</bold>. Sch&#x000fc;ffner Dots). <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> schizonts contain<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>12 to 24 merozoites, almost fill the entire cell,&#x000a0;and also contain Sch&#x000fc;ffner dots.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27310.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>The typical incubation period for <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> malaria is 12 to 17 days, but relapse may occur up to 2 years later from dormant hypnozoites.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r15">[15]</xref> Classic clinical signs and symptoms of malaria include fever, headache, nausea,&#x000a0;vomiting, body aches, anemia, and jaundice. Making a clinical diagnosis of malaria is sometimes challenging because of the nonspecific symptoms and overlap of signs and symptoms with other febrile illnesses.</p>
        <p>Due to the parasite life cycle, patients with <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> malaria tend to have paroxysmal fevers every approximately 42 to 56 hours.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r16">[16]</xref> Thus,&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> is sometimes referred to as "tertian fever." Anemia is the most common presenting symptom&#x000a0;among adults and children living in&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P vivax-</italic>endemic&#x000a0;areas. Unlike falciparum malaria, vivax malaria does not tend to cause sequestration; thus, multisystem organ failure is less common. However, cases of severe <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> malaria have been reported.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r18">[18]</xref> Acute respiratory distress syndrome can occur secondary to the host's inflammatory immune response to parasitemia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r16">[16]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27310.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>The&#x000a0;WHO adjusted its guidelines in 2010 to recommend that all patients suspected of having malaria undergo testing before initiating antimalarial treatment. The 2 primary diagnostic modalities used to test for malaria in the clinical setting are light microscopy and rapid diagnostic tests (RDTs). Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) testing is also available, though this test is more commonly used in research settings. Additionally, PCR can be used to confirm malaria species after establishing a diagnosis.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Blood Smear</bold>
</p>
        <p>Direct visualization of <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> parasites on Giemsa-stained blood smears by light microscopy is considered the gold standard for the diagnosis of <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> malaria. Three sets of both thick and thin smears should be obtained. Due to the large number of erythrocytes visualized, thick smears are more sensitive and useful for detecting parasitemia. Thin smears allow better visualization of parasites, which aids in speciation. Thin smears can also be used to calculate parasite density. Light microscopy has good sensitivity and is low-cost. Limitations to microscopy include the necessity for trained and experienced laboratory technicians, access to a microscope, and electricity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;Compared with&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P falciparum</italic>, the number of parasites circulating in the blood of a person infected with <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> malaria is typically lower, making it more challenging to diagnose. Also, the dormant liver hypnozoite form is undetectable with current diagnostic tools.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Rapid Diagnostic Test (RDT)&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Since the 1990s, RDTs have become increasingly popular diagnostic tools, especially in resource-limited settings.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r19">[19]</xref> RDTs detect 1 or more <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium</italic> antigens in the blood. They offer the benefit of being fast, yielding results in about 15 to 20 minutes, and do not require a laboratory technician skilled at making and reading blood smears. RDTs are more sensitive at higher parasite loads and may be falsely negative at lower levels of parasitemia, which also makes them less sensitive in children younger than 5 years of age.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>Worldwide, several RDTs can diagnose&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P vivax&#x000a0;</italic>with high sensitivity and specificity; however, for&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P vivax in</italic>&#x000a0;particular, assay&#x000a0;sensitivity depends on the degree of parasitemia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;Only 1 RDT is approved by the FDA for the United States: BinaxNOW (Abbott), which detects&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic>&#x000a0;via pan-species aldolase and therefore&#x000a0;does not identify the <italic toggle="yes">vivax</italic> species.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Studies evaluating the utility of RDTs in detecting <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> have shown significantly lower sensitivities; the BinaxNOW assay&#x02019;s sensitivity and specificity for <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> is 69% and 99%, respectively.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;Authors of a 2020 Cochrane Systematic Review of 10 studies examining 6 RDT brands found that the CareStart Malaria Pf/Pv Combo test (pooling the results of 4 studies) had a 99% sensitivity and 99% specificity for <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>RDTs can remain positive for a week or longer after successful treatment of malaria.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r28">[28]</xref> In the United States, standard practice is to confirm all RDTs with light microscopy; the FDA specifies that the BinaxNOW RDT does not eliminate the need to examine blood smears for the presence of malaria parasites due to false negatives associated with low levels of parasitemia.<bold/></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27310.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>The treatment of <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> malaria requires 2 agents. The first agent is to eliminate the acute blood-stage infection, and the second is to clear dormant hypnozoites in the liver to prevent future relapses. Additionally, the geographic location of malaria acquisition is an important historical factor in guiding appropriate therapy, given emerging drug resistance (see Table.&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P Vivax</italic>&#x000a0;Malaria Regimens). Papua New Guinea and Indonesia are the primary areas of chloroquine resistance in&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic>, though other areas include the Solomon Islands, Vanuatu, Cambodia, Eritrea, and South Sudan.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r29">[29]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-27310.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table.&#x000a0;P Vivax&#x000a0;Malaria Regimens</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Chloroquine Sensitivity</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Treatment Regimens</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Chloroquine-sensitive areas</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Chloroquine phosphate: 600 mg base (=1,000 mg salt) orally immediately, followed by 300 mg base (=500 mg salt) orally at 6, 24, and 48 hours. Total dose: 1,500 mg base (=2,500 mg salt)</p>
<p><bold>OR</bold></p>
<p>Hydroxychloroquine: 620 mg base (=800 mg salt) orally immediately, followed by 310 mg base (=400 mg salt) orally at 6, 24, and 48 hours. Total dose: 1550 mg base (=2000 mg salt)&#x000a0;</p>
<p><bold>AND</bold></p>
<p>After the first or second day of treatment for the blood stage, primaquine phosphate: 30 mg base orally once daily for 14 days. Patients who&#x000a0;weigh more than 70 kg will need to extend therapy by 1 day for each 5 kg above 70 kg. The preferred alternative is tafenoquine 300 mg as a single dose because adherence is better. Screening for glucose-6-phosphate-dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency before prescribing primaquine or tafenoquine is required.[CDC.&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.cdc.gov/malaria/hcp/clinical-guidance/treatment-uncomplicated.html">Treatment of Uncomplicated Malaria</ext-link>. 2024]</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Chloroquine-resistant areas&#x000a0;</bold>(eg,&#x000a0;Papua New Guinea and Indonesia)</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Artemether-lumefantrine, atovaquone-proguanil, or quinine&#x000a0;</p>
<p><bold>AND</bold></p>
<p>Tetracycline, doxycycline, mefloquine, or clindamycin for pregnant women and children younger than 8 years. Due to the potential for severe adverse effects, mefloquine is reserved for last.</p>
<p><bold>AND</bold></p>
<p>After the first or second day of treatment for the blood stage, primaquine phosphate is administered at the above dosage. Tafenoquine is not effective in preventing recurrence if the blood stage infection was treated with a nonchloroquine regimen.</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Additionally, treatment with Artemisinin-based Combination Therapy (ACT) plus<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>primaquine is an option that unifies&#x000a0;malaria treatment and can be beneficial in areas where both <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">P falciparum</italic> are present, when speciation is not possible, or when resistance patterns are fluctuating.</p>
        <p>Because of the dormant liver phase of <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic>, primaquine or tafenoquine must also be added to treatment regimens to treat the hypnozoite phase. Two scenarios in which primaquine or tafenoquine are contraindicated include patients with G6PD deficiency and pregnant women. Also, tafenoquine is not approved for persons younger than 16 years. In patients with G6PD deficiency, primaquine may cause severe hemolytic anemia. Decisions regarding its use in this patient population must be made on a case-by-case basis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r30">[30]</xref> In pregnant women, primaquine and tafenoquine are contraindicated because of the risk for fetal hemolysis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r31">[31]</xref> Pregnant patients with <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> infections should be maintained on chloroquine chemoprophylaxis for the duration of their pregnancy and primaquine or tafenoquine treatment delayed until after delivery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Severe Malaria</bold>
</p>
        <p>Fortunately, severe forms of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> malaria are less common than in infections with <italic toggle="yes">P falciparum</italic>; nevertheless, severe <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> cases, including death, do occur. Severe <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> malaria is most common in young children and in those with comorbid conditions. Severe malaria is associated with higher parasitemia load. Where laboratory testing is available, severe malaria is defined by the WHO as patients with hypoglycemia (&#x0003c;2.2 mmol/l or &#x0003c;40 mg/dL),&#x000a0;metabolic acidosis (plasma bicarbonate &#x0003c;15 mmol/L), severe normocytic anemia (hemoglobin &#x0003c;5 g/dL, packed cell volume &#x0003c;15% in children; &#x0003c;7 g/dL, packed cell volume &#x0003c;20% in adults), hemoglobinuria, elevated lactic acid&#x000a0;(lactate &#x0003e;5 mmol/L), renal impairment (serum creatinine &#x0003e;265 &#x003bc;mol/L), and pulmonary edema.[WHO. (<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/guidelines-for-malaria">Guidelines for the Treatment of Malaria</ext-link>. 2025]</p>
        <p>Clinical signs of severe malaria include altered mental status, severe generalized weakness, multiple convulsions, tachypnea and respiratory distress, pulmonary edema, signs of shock, jaundice, abnormal bleeding, and kidney injury.[WHO. (<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/guidelines-for-malaria">Guidelines for the Treatment of Malaria</ext-link>. 2025]&#x000a0;For the treatment of severe malaria worldwide, the WHO and CDC recommend the use of intravenous (IV) artesunate (2.4 mg/kg IV or IM at 0, 12, and 24 hours then daily) for nonpregnant and pregnant patients.[WHO. (<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/guidelines-for-malaria">Guidelines for the Treatment of Malaria</ext-link>. 2025]&#x000a0;Multiple studies have shown improved efficacy and safety of artesunate in the treatment of severe malaria as compared to previously used intravenous quinidine.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r35">[35]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>If IV artesunate is not available, oral or rectal artesunate&#x000a0;should be given until IV artesunate can be acquired. Patients on treatment for severe malaria should have 1 set of blood smears (thick and thin smear) performed on admission and every 12 to 24 hours until a negative result is reported (no <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium</italic> parasites detected). Patients should remain on IV artesunate until parasitemia is less than 1% or for 7 days. Artemether-lumefantrine is the preferred follow-up treatment, but adequate alternatives are atovaquone-proguanil, dihydroartemisinin-piperaquine (not approved by the FDA for the United States), quinine plus doxycycline or clindamycin, or mefloquine.[CDC. (<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.cdc.gov/malaria/hcp/clinical-guidance/treatment-of-severe-malaria.html">Treatment of Severe Malaria</ext-link>. 2024]</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27310.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Differential diagnoses that should also be considered when evaluating patients suspected of having malaria include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Dengue fever</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Chikungunya</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Typhoid Fever</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sepsis from bacteremia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Bacterial meningitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Viral meningitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Viral hemorrhagic fever</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Leptospirosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rickettsial infections</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Brucellosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Influenza</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>SARS-CoV-2</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Viral hepatitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Yellow fever</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Acute schistosomiasis</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27310.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>If appropriately recognized and treated, <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium vivax</italic> malaria has a good prognosis and causes significantly fewer complications than falciparum malaria. However, the fact that severe disease can occur has been well established, and if the liver stage is left untreated, it can relapse years later.&#x000a0;(Please refer to the <bold>Complications</bold> section for more information)</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27310.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>While classically,&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> causes lower parasitemia and less severe disease than <italic toggle="yes">P falciparum</italic>,&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> can still cause severe disease and death. Case reports have demonstrated <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> causing cerebral malaria, renal failure, acute respiratory distress, and shock.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>Another rare complication is splenic rupture, which carries a very high mortality rate of up to 80%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r36">[36]</xref>&#x000a0;Pregnant women are at increased risk of adverse outcomes from all forms of malaria, and <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> is no exception. Malaria in pregnancy is associated with higher levels of parasitemia and increases the risk of prematurity, miscarriage, low birth weight, neonatal or maternal death.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r32">[32]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27310.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Malaria prevention takes a multifaceted approach. For travelers, adherence to appropriate malaria prophylaxis medications is key. Also, wearing appropriate clothing (long sleeves and pants) and using DEET and permethrin have been shown to reduce the risk of malaria transmission greatly.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r37">[37]</xref> Additionally, recognizing that <italic toggle="yes">Anopheles</italic> mosquitoes generally bite around dawn and dusk, appropriate behavioral modifications may help prevent infection as well. For patients living or staying in endemic regions, the use of treated mosquito nets correlates with decreased infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r38">[38]</xref>&#x000a0;This population-level control strategy has permeated the majority of households in recent years, with an estimated 70% of households in sub-Saharan Africa owning at least 1 treated net by 2022, a figure that has remained stable since 2015.[WHO. <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.who.int/teams/global-malaria-programme/reports/world-malaria-report-2023">World Malaria Report 2023</ext-link>]</p>
        <p>Some studies have demonstrated the value of treating other household items with permethrin, eg, baby wraps.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r39">[39]</xref>&#x000a0;Additionally, indoor residual insecticide spraying to control <italic toggle="yes">Anopheles</italic> mosquito populations has been shown to significantly reduce malaria, though only 1.8% of populations in sub-Saharan Africa were protected in 2022.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r40">[40]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r41">[41]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Chemoprophylaxis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Most chemoprophylaxis recommendations target <italic toggle="yes">P falciparum.</italic>&#x000a0;The WHO recommends <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> chemoprevention with chloroquine in pregnant and lactating women in <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> endemic areas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r42">[42]</xref> Drugs recommended for chemoprophylaxis for travelers to geographic areas endemic for <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> include chloroquine or mefloquine (only in areas with no resistance), atovaquone-proguanil, doxycycline, and primaquine or tafenoquine (only for persons without G6PD deficiency, nonpregnant, non-nursing).[CDC. <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/keywords/edit/186573/Choosing a Drug to Prevent Malaria">Choosing a Drug to Prevent Malaria</ext-link>. 2024]&#x000a0;However, only primaquine or tafenoquine (both 9-aminoquinolines) eliminate the hypnozoite (asexual form or liver stage) form of the protozoan, but they cannot be used in G6PD-deficient persons.</p>
        <p>In geographic areas where G6PD deficiency and <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> endemicity coincide, eg, Southeast Asia, they are of limited utility. An alternative antimalarial drug that kills hypnozoites while not associated with hemolytic anemia in G6PD-deficient persons is naphthoquine. A recent randomized controlled trial conducted along the China-Myanmar border concluded that monthly naphthoquine-azithromycin was highly efficacious and well-tolerated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r43">[43]</xref></p>
        <p>Additionally, 2 different vaccines were approved for children in 2021 for use in high-prevalence malaria settings, but with a focus on protection from&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium falciparum</italic>&#x000a0;infection. Vaccines against <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic>&#x000a0;have been researched for years, but none have advanced to phase 3 human efficacy trials.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r42">[42]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27310.r44">[44]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27310.s14" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Factors that should be kept in mind when managing malaria caused by&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium vivax</italic> include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium vivax</italic> is the most widespread malaria species, with predominance in Latin America and Southeast Asia.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The blood smear is the&#x000a0;gold-standard diagnostic tool for malaria, but in resource-limited clinical settings, rapid diagnostic tests (RDTs) are a reasonable alternative, though less sensitive for<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;P&#x000a0;vivax.</italic></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Treatment decisions need to account for the severity of the disease and local patterns of drug resistance.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The uncomplicated disease is treatable with chloroquine or hydroxychloroquine in combination with primaquine or tafenoquine.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>In severe disease, IV artesunate is the treatment of choice worldwide, regardless of <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium</italic> species.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>To avoid a relapse, <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> treatment and prophylaxis must always end with treatment of hypnozoites using primaquine or tafenoquine in G6PD competent persons.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27310.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Malaria is a life-threatening parasitic infection caused by <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium</italic> species, with <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium vivax</italic> notable for its wide geographic distribution and ability to cause relapsing disease due to dormant hepatic hypnozoites. Transmission occurs via <italic toggle="yes">Anopheles</italic> mosquitoes, with parasites progressing from liver-stage schizonts to erythrocytic infection, producing cyclical fevers, anemia, and systemic symptoms. Although parasitemia is typically lower than in <italic toggle="yes">P falciparum</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">P vivax</italic> can cause severe complications, including acute respiratory distress syndrome and severe anemia. Diagnosis relies on microscopy as the gold standard, supplemented by rapid diagnostic tests, though sensitivity may be limited at low parasite densities. Management requires dual therapy targeting both blood-stage infection and hypnozoites, with attention to regional drug resistance and screening for G6PD deficiency before primaquine or tafenoquine use.</p>
        <p>Interprofessional collaboration is essential to optimize outcomes and reduce relapse and complications. Physicians and advanced practice providers lead diagnostic evaluation and initiate evidence-based therapy, while primary care clinicians support early recognition and longitudinal follow-up. Nurses monitor clinical status, ensure treatment adherence, and provide patient education on symptom recognition and prevention. Pharmacists verify appropriate antimalarial selection, dosing, drug interactions, and safety considerations, including G6PD-related risks. Laboratory professionals ensure accurate and timely diagnostic testing and speciation. Coordinated communication among team members supports shared decision-making, timely referral for severe disease, and adherence to treatment protocols, improving patient safety, reducing transmission, and enhancing quality of care.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27310.s16">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
